doc_id: router-patterns
title: Router Patterns
project: ai-workflow
version_hint: "2024-2025"
kind: rest_api_router_standards
description: Standards for building API routers ensuring consistency, testability, and proper OpenAPI documentation.
scope: Covers router organization, configuration, handler structure, dependency injection, validation, response handling, error handling, OpenAPI documentation, registration, and anti-patterns.
primary_runtime_references[3]:
  - path: app/api/v1/router.py
    role: Versioned API router registration.
  - path: app/api/v1/endpoints/
    role: Endpoint implementations directory.
  - path: app/contracts/errors.py
    role: Error response contracts.
transitional_note: References to .md files (dependency-patterns.md, exception-patterns.md) are transitional and will be updated to .toon filenames once those documents are converted in later phases.
related_pattern_docs[4]:
  - api-patterns.toon
  - service-patterns.toon
  - dependency-patterns.md
  - exception-patterns.md
sections[10]:
  - id: router-organization
    index: 1
    title: APIRouter organization
    category: standard
    summary: Organize routers by domain or resource for clarity and maintainability.
    sample_code:
      description: Minimal router instantiation and route definition.
      imports[1]: fastapi.APIRouter
      code: "router = APIRouter()\n\n@router.get(\"\")\nasync def list_items():\n    ..."
    items[5]:
      - id: organization.granularity
        type: rule
        text: Create one router per domain or resource (e.g., users.py, items.py, orders.py).
      - id: organization.instantiation
        type: rule
        text: Use APIRouter() instances; never attach routes directly to the app object in endpoint files.
      - id: organization.grouping
        type: rule
        text: Group related endpoints within the same router file for discoverability.
      - id: organization.versioned-registration
        type: rule
        text: Register each router with the versioned API router so the application factory can apply the /api/{version} prefix and shared dependencies.
      - id: organization.trailing-slashes
        type: rule
        text: Use canonical paths without a trailing slash; allow FastAPI redirect behavior to handle variants while documenting only the non-slashed form.
  - id: router-configuration
    index: 2
    title: Router configuration
    category: standard
    summary: Configure routers to reduce repetition and ensure consistent API structure.
    items[4]:
      - id: config.prefix
        type: rule
        text: "Set the prefix argument at router level (e.g., prefix=\"/items\") when including the router; prefer registration time so paths follow the no-trailing-slash convention."
      - id: config.tags
        type: rule
        text: Use tags for grouping endpoints in OpenAPI UI; apply consistently either at router registration time (preferred) or on individual route decorators, but do not mix both patterns.
      - id: config.dependencies
        type: rule
        text: Apply shared dependencies (authentication, authorization, rate limiting, request logging) at router level when they apply to all endpoints.
      - id: config.responses
        type: rule
        text: When many routes share the same response shapes, define common responses at router or registration time using shared contracts from the contracts module.
  - id: handler-structure
    index: 3
    title: Route handler structure
    category: standard
    summary: Keep route handlers thin; their responsibility is to validate input, call service methods, and return responses.
    sample_code:
      description: Standard pattern for a thin route handler delegating to a service.
      imports[3]: fastapi.APIRouter,typing.Annotated,fastapi.Depends
      components[1]:
        - type: route
          method: GET
          path: "/{item_id}"
          response_model: ItemResponse
          handler_params: "item_id: int, service: Annotated[ItemService, Depends(get_item_service)]"
          body: "return await service.get_item(item_id)"
    items[4]:
      - id: handler.thin
        type: rule
        text: Keep route handlers thin; they should only validate, call services, and return responses.
      - id: handler.validation
        type: rule
        text: Let Pydantic and FastAPI handle input validation automatically via type hints and models.
      - id: handler.service-call
        type: rule
        text: Call the appropriate service layer method for business logic.
      - id: handler.return
        type: rule
        text: Return Pydantic models; let FastAPI handle serialization.
  - id: dependency-injection
    index: 4
    title: Dependency injection in routers
    category: standard
    summary: Use FastAPI dependency injection for services, settings, database sessions, and user context.
    sample_code:
      description: Dependency injection using Annotated type aliases for clean signatures.
      imports[3]: typing.Annotated,fastapi.Depends,app.core.dependencies.get_settings
      snippets[3]:
        - name: type_alias
          code: "ItemServiceDep = Annotated[ItemService, Depends(get_item_service)]"
        - name: handler_with_deps
          code: "async def create_item(item: ItemCreate, service: ItemServiceDep, settings: Annotated[AppSettings, Depends(get_settings)]): ..."
        - name: bad_direct_db_access
          code: "# Avoid: direct DB access from routers\nasync def create_item(item: ItemCreate, db: AsyncSession = Depends(get_db)): ..."
    items[4]:
      - id: di.annotated
        type: rule
        text: Use Annotated type aliases for cleaner function signatures.
      - id: di.service-injection
        type: rule
        text: Inject services rather than repositories or DB connections directly into routers.
      - id: di.settings-injection
        type: rule
        text: Inject configuration via Depends(get_settings) instead of reading environment variables inside route handlers.
      - id: di.shared-providers
        type: rule
        text: Keep get_*_service, get_settings, and other provider functions in dedicated dependency modules for reuse across routers, background tasks, and tests.
  - id: request-validation
    index: 5
    title: Request validation
    category: standard
    summary: Use Pydantic models and FastAPI parameter types for comprehensive request validation.
    sample_code:
      description: Request validation using Query, Header, and type hints with constraints.
      imports[2]: typing.Annotated,fastapi.Query
      code: "async def list_items(page: Annotated[int, Query(ge=1)] = 1, page_size: Annotated[int, Query(ge=1, le=100)] = 20, user_agent: Annotated[str | None, Header()] = None) -> ItemsPage: ..."
    items[3]:
      - id: validation.pydantic-models
        type: rule
        text: Use Pydantic models for request bodies (defined in contracts).
      - id: validation.path-query
        type: rule
        text: Use Path, Query, Header with strict types for path and query parameters.
      - id: validation.constraints
        type: rule
        text: Use Field for validation rules (min_length, regex, etc.) in Pydantic models, not in router logic.
  - id: response-handling
    index: 6
    title: Response handling
    category: standard
    summary: Always specify response models and appropriate status codes for type safety and documentation.
    sample_code:
      description: Response handling with explicit response_model and status_code.
      imports[2]: fastapi.APIRouter,fastapi.status
      snippets[2]:
        - name: create_route
          code: "@router.post(\"/\", response_model=ItemResponse, status_code=201)\nasync def create_item(item: ItemCreate, ...): ..."
        - name: delete_route
          code: "@router.delete(\"/{id}\", response_model=None, status_code=204)\nasync def delete_item(id: int, ...): ..."
    items[4]:
      - id: response.model-required
        type: rule
        text: Always specify response_model in the decorator for output data filtering, validation, and OpenAPI schema derivation.
      - id: response.status-codes
        type: rule
        text: Explicitly set status_code for non-200 success cases (e.g., 201 Created).
      - id: response.empty
        type: rule
        text: For 204 No Content, use response_model=None and status_code=204.
      - id: response.serializer
        type: rule
        text: Prefer the global default response class from the application factory; override only when a different content type or serialization behavior is required.
  - id: error-handling
    index: 7
    title: Error handling in routers
    category: standard
    summary: Let services raise domain exceptions; rely on global handlers for HTTP translation.
    items[4]:
      - id: errors.domain-exceptions
        type: rule
        text: Let the service layer raise specific domain exceptions (e.g., ItemNotFound, DuplicateItem).
      - id: errors.infrastructure
        type: rule
        text: Let infrastructure components raise their own typed errors rather than converting them to HTTP responses.
      - id: errors.global-handlers
        type: rule
        text: Rely on global exception handlers to translate domain and infrastructure exceptions into HTTP responses.
      - id: errors.router-specific
        type: rule
        text: Only raise HTTPException directly in routers for router-specific concerns such as authentication failures, authorization checks, and rate limiting.
  - id: openapi-documentation
    index: 8
    title: OpenAPI documentation
    category: standard
    summary: Maximize utility of auto-generated API docs with comprehensive route metadata.
    sample_code:
      description: Comprehensive OpenAPI documentation on a route decorator.
      imports[2]: fastapi.APIRouter,fastapi.status
      components[1]:
        - type: route
          method: GET
          path: "/{item_id}"
          response_model: ItemResponse
          status_code: status.HTTP_200_OK
          summary: Get item by ID
          description: Retrieves full item details including inventory status.
          responses: "200: {\"description\": \"Item retrieved successfully\"}, 400: VALIDATION_ERROR_RESPONSE, 403: {\"description\": \"Not authorized to view this item\"}, 404: {\"description\": \"Item not found\"}"
    items[3]:
      - id: openapi.summary
        type: rule
        text: Every route must define a concise summary that describes its primary behavior.
      - id: openapi.description
        type: rule
        text: Complex routes must define a description explaining important details, side effects, and edge cases (Markdown supported).
      - id: openapi.responses
        type: rule
        text: Document all relevant responses (success and error) using the responses parameter so status codes, schemas, and examples are explicit.
  - id: router-registration
    index: 9
    title: Router registration
    category: standard
    summary: Centralize router registration in the main API router file for maintainability.
    sample_code:
      description: Central router registration pattern.
      imports[2]: fastapi.APIRouter,some_module.items_router
      code: "api_v1_router = APIRouter()\napi_v1_router.include_router(items_router, prefix=\"/items\", tags=[\"Items\"])"
    items[4]:
      - id: registration.include-router
        type: rule
        text: Use api_router.include_router() to register routers.
      - id: registration.configuration
        type: rule
        text: Set prefix and tags at registration time to keep endpoint files clean.
      - id: registration.version-prefix
        type: rule
        text: Apply the /api/{version} prefix in the application factory so routers remain version-agnostic.
      - id: registration.route-ordering
        type: rule
        text: Register more specific routers before generic or catch-all routers to avoid shadowing.
  - id: anti-patterns
    index: 10
    title: Anti-patterns to avoid
    category: anti_patterns
    summary: Common pitfalls that violate router standards.
    items[7]:
      - id: anti.business-logic
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Business logic in routers; do not perform calculations, complex conditionals, or raw DB queries in route handlers.
      - id: anti.direct-db
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Direct DB access; avoid db.execute(...) in routers; use a service.
      - id: anti.manual-json
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Manual JSON response; avoid returning dictionaries or JSONResponse manually unless absolutely necessary.
      - id: anti.missing-response-model
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Missing response model; always define the return schema via response_model.
      - id: anti.inconsistent-docs
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Inconsistent documentation or error schemas; do not omit summary, description, or error responses, and do not handcraft unique error shapes per route.
      - id: anti.bypass-error-handling
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Bypassing global error handling; do not raise HTTP errors directly from services or infrastructure layers.
      - id: anti.blocking-code
        type: anti_pattern
        text: Blocking code; do not use sync I/O methods in async def handlers or mix heavy synchronous work into async routes without proper offloading.
